<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Isandlwana</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1879">1879</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-zulu-war">Anglo-Zulu War</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Isandlwana was fought on 22 January 1879, at the start of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-zulu-war">Anglo-Zulu War</a>, when some 20,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zulu-kingdom">Zulu</a> warriors overran the camp of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederic-thesiger-2nd-baron-chelmsford">Lord Chelmsford</a>'s centre column below Isandlwana hill. More than 1,300 British soldiers and African auxiliaries were killed. It was one of the worst defeats suffered by a British army against an indigenous force and caused a political shock in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-isandlwana.jpg" alt="Battle of Isandlwana" width="320" height="200" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Charles Edwin Fripp · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Isandhlwana.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>In 1877 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bartle-frere">Sir Henry Bartle Frere</a> was appointed High Commissioner for Southern Africa to bring about a confederation in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>, as had been done in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>. The independent South African Republic and the Kingdom of Zululand stood in the way, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-empire">British Empire</a> meant to overcome both by force. On 11 December 1878 Bartle Frere, on his own initiative and without the approval of the British government, presented the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zulu-kingdom">Zulu</a> king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cetshwayo">Cetshwayo</a> with an ultimatum he could not possibly meet. When it expired a month later he ordered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederic-thesiger-2nd-baron-chelmsford">Lord Chelmsford</a> to invade Zululand. Chelmsford planned a five-pronged invasion, then settled on three columns, the centre one under his own command. It crossed the Buffalo River at Rorke's Drift on 11 January 1879.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>On 22 January Chelmsford was in the field looking for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zulu-kingdom">Zulu</a> army, which had outmanoeuvred him. About 20,000 Zulu warriors, commanded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ntshingwayo-khoza">Ntshingwayo</a> kaMahole Khoza and Mavumengwana kaNdlela Ntuli, attacked part of the main column, some 1,800 British, colonial and native troops with about 350 civilians, at Isandlwana. The Zulus carried mainly assegais and cow-hide shields, with some <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/musket">muskets</a> and old rifles. The British had Martini-Henry rifles, two 7-pounder guns and a rocket battery.</p><p>Scouts under Lieutenant Charles Raw found the main <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zulu-kingdom">Zulu</a> force at about 11:00 am, and it at once attacked. Attacked on the flanks by the Zulu formation known as the horns and chest of the buffalo, the British could not concentrate. A solar eclipse occurred at 2:30 pm. More than 1,300 troops were killed, including all those on the forward firing line. Evidence shows several last stands, one by about 150 men, and no quarter was given to the wounded. Zulu dead are estimated at 1,000 to 3,000.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The victory ended the first British invasion of Zululand, and the British Army had suffered its worst defeat by an indigenous force with far inferior weapons. It led to a more aggressive approach, a heavily reinforced second invasion and the end of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cetshwayo">Cetshwayo</a>'s hopes of a negotiated peace. Chelmsford blamed Colonel Anthony Durnford, though there is no evidence that he disobeyed an order; Wolseley thought the aim was to protect Chelmsford. Recent historians such as Lock and Quantrill place responsibility with Chelmsford.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Isandlwana” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The army’s greatest victory came on 22 January 1879 at the <b>Battle of Isandlwana</b>, where some 20,000 Zulu overran a British camp and killed more than 1,300 soldiers and auxiliaries.</blockquote><a href="/empire/zulu-kingdom">Zulu Kingdom · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>It is also heard in Zulu Dawn, about the <b>Battle of Isandlwana</b>, and in The Englishman Who Went up a Hill but Came down a Mountain (1995).</blockquote><a href="/songs/men-of-harlech">Men of Harlech</a></li></ul></section>
